					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="2303803"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1531055004 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">08 Jul 2018 13:03</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3866546">
									

<blockquote>
<p>So you mean to say that literally no one is straight there? I get that, this is a webcomic and literally anything can happen but, its really jarring to have everyone be LGBT and not have a single straight person in there.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Well, the thing about that&#8230; You really don't know if any given stranger on the street is LGBT or not. Not all LGBT people are out to the public or LGBT in a visually noticeable way. The same goes for fictional characters. We only know that these characters are all LGBT because the author said so. Otherwise, we would only pick up on the lesbian couple (edit: and the trans zombie but their line was so subtle that I almost missed it) in it. So it's less about &quot;there are no cisgender heterosexual people&quot; and more about &quot;the reader would have assumed some of these people are straight, but looks can be deceiving and it's actually a group of LGBT friends&quot;.</p>
<p>Point being, it ain't statistically unlikely for a group of people close with each other to share a demographic like that. If this were a random selection of people off the street, of course it'd be unrealistic for them to all be LGBT. But these people know each other and clearly have shared struggles, so it makes sense to me that they all have that similarity. I see it happen in RL friend groups all the time.</p>

							</div>

								
