				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="2303803"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1531054271 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">08 Jul 2018 12:51</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3866534">
									

<p>I feel like this hit a pitfall common with format screws, which is that so much focus was put on the format screw aspect that the perceived necessity of the screw took precedence over logic. I don't see a reason for this <em>entire page</em> to be a comic. If people reading about the characters helps them, then it makes sense to have a few panels in the addenda where people can perceive them, but having the <em>whole thing</em> be a comic just didn't make sense in-universe to me. I kept expecting the reason for it to be some sort of infohazard or whatever, but it was actually just &quot;they let us write our own article because it emotionally benefits us&quot; and that's just kind of flimsy. There's no reason the Foundation would let a document be this confusing and indirect when there's no info- or textual hazard at play in it. The characters recognize and cite that it's confusing for the researchers, so why hasn't a researcher formatted their article and left their panels in a different section of the article?</p>
<p>I upvoted at first, but the more I typed for this comment, the more I noticed the issues I have with it. I'm cool with the concept and the characters, but I don't see any compelling in-universe reasons that the format is 100% comic. So novote, but let me know if I'm missing something here and I may reconsider.</p>

							</div>

								
